response:
Sure.  Loops help your program execute instructions repeatedly.  For example, to go through all the numbers from 1 through 10, you could write:

for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
    // process i
}

Alternatively, you could add more code to the loop to process the number in a different way after it’s processed:

for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
    int j = i * 2;
    if (j > 10) {
        break;
    }
    // process j
}

The first version prints eight lines of text, and the second version keeps the same loop structure but adds the condition in the first block of code, and then keeps processing new numbers that exceed 10.

